WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.870
I am back here with the legendary Iman.

2
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.370
And Iman Cosmetics launched

3
00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:10.180
in 1994.

4
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.930
And we actually have Sharon Chuter

5
00:00:16.706 --> 00:00:20.020
on the program, who did the Pull Up For Change,

6
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.550
the hashtag, which you participated in.

7
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.540
We luckily at FLOWER, you know, participated in,

8
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.100
'cause it's such an important thing

9
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.450
to be a part of what she's doing.

10
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.870
And she has UOMA Beauty.

11
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.980
And it was funny because everybody put in their thing,

12
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.330
what percentage of women and black women

13
00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.770
and what everybody had at their companies.

14
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.770
And yours was like 100% women, 85% black,

15
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.558
been doing this for 25 years plus.

16
00:00:49.558 --> 00:00:53.160
And I read a story about how you showed up to a shoot

17
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.337
and the makeup artist said, like,

18
00:00:54.337 --> 00:00:55.747
"Did you bring your own foundation,

19
00:00:55.747 --> 00:00:58.830
"because we just don't have it?"

20
00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.671
Yup.

21
00:00:59.671 --> 00:01:02.880
And, was there several catalysts that got you

22
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.170
to launch Iman Cosmetics that was so important,

23
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.463
necessary, and groundbreaking at that time?

24
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.780
Yes, actually, because it really happened

25
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.400
at that photo shoot.

26
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.050
It was in 1975.

27
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.450 line:15%
It was my first photo shoot and it was for American Vogue.

28
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.590 line:15%
And I have never worn heels.

29
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.500 line:15%
I've never worn makeup before I arrived

30
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.460 line:15%
in the United States.

31
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.890
I was studying political science.

32
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.390
I've never seen fashion magazines.

33
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.230
I had no idea that there was anything called fashion

34
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.130
that existed, you know, in the world.

35
00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.570
So at that photo shoot, I walked in

36
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.950
and there was another model on the shoot

37
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.090
and she was Caucasian.

38
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.455
And the makeup artist asked me,

39
00:01:44.455 --> 00:01:47.170
and by the way, I was then 19 years old.

40
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.417
He asked me specifically,

41
00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:50.770
"Did you bring your own foundation?"

42
00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.670
But it wasn't lost on me that he didn't ask that question

43
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.110
to the Caucasian girl.

44
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.180
So I thought something,

45
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.733
because I had no idea what he was talking about.

46
00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:00.430
I didn't even know what foundation was.

47
00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.950
So then he proceeded to mix and match some products

48
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.323
and put it on my face.

49
00:02:05.755 --> 00:02:07.999
And I looked in the mirror and I looked gray.

50
00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:11.080
I looked horribly gray, my skin.

51
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.110
And so what saved my career,

52
00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.550
because that was my first shoot.

53
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.130
What saved my career was that our photographs

54
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.880
were actually black and white.

55
00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.210
And those black and whites hide a multitude of sins.

56
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.270
Interesting.

57
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.100
But what I did was that I made it my job

58
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:31.110
to find something that was suitable for my skin.

59
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.500
So I went to all the department stores.

60
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:34.860
I went to Woolworth.

61
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.171
You're too young to remember Woolworth.

62
00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:38.750
Oh no, of course I rocked it at Woolworths, too.

63
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.570
I came out of the womb, 10 with a martini in 1975.

64
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.222
So I was new to the planet.

65
00:02:45.222 --> 00:02:47.221
I love you for that, I love you for that,

66
00:02:47.221 --> 00:02:49.437
I love you for that (laughs).

67
00:02:49.437 --> 00:02:50.590
I did.

68
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.610
So, I mixed and mixed product and put it on my face.

69
00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.420
And I took a Polaroid camera and I took pictures,

70
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.690
so I could see how will it translate.

71
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.810
That was the seed that was implanted in my head,

72
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.730
that I have to take control of my own image.

73
00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.410
You know, we were talking about language,

74
00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:12.420
but in fashion and beauty, you know,

75
00:03:14.878 --> 00:03:16.650
our power is how we look.

76
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.760
Our currency is how our images look.

77
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.760
So I wanted to make sure that I took control of that

78
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.803
and that I could put myself at the best forward light

79
00:03:26.803 --> 00:03:29.240
you know, and I never went to a fashion shoot

80
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.200
without a foundation from then on.

81
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.100
And you did.

82
00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:37.150
I retired in 1989 and I created in 1994

83
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.140
Iman Cosmetics for women with skin of color.

84
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.300
And I say skin of color, because before I started

85
00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.670
my makeup, everybody, especially black,

86
00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.265
were called just women of color.

87
00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:48.098
Yeah.

88
00:03:48.098 --> 00:03:49.750
And even now, when you think about women of color,

89
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.880
you only think about black women.

90
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.170
But I wanted a new language of beauty,

91
00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.446
of how to include everybody in.

92
00:03:57.446 --> 00:03:59.980
Because there are Filipinos who are as dark as I am.

93
00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.190
There are Puerto Ricans who are, you know, the same color,

94
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.620
Brazilians who are light, so the whole world.

95
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.650
I was not interested in the ethnicity,

96
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.013
I was interested in the spectrum of colors

97
00:04:09.013 --> 00:04:10.960
that the whole world comes in.

98
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.870
Well, you have been a pioneer in so many areas.

99
00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.310
And I thank you for finding the words and the solves

100
00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:23.490
in the beauty industry for women to empower,

101
00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:28.260
properly address and solve what so many women need.

102
00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.120
You are an icon with an incredible vision and soul

103
00:04:34.549 --> 00:04:38.350
and proactiveness, which I think is the most insane thing.

104
00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.730
And by the way, speaking of color, fun fact.

105
00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.803
You have a dog named Max?

106
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:44.593
Yes.

107
00:04:44.593 --> 00:04:48.350
who has different colored eyes like your beloved,

108
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:51.810
the legendary David Bowie, which I was reading a book

109
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.710
to my children the other night about him.

110
00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:59.010
And I had never known that he got punched

111
00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.083
and that was how his eye changed color.

112
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.530
Exactly, except Max was born like that.

113
00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.410
My dog was born like that.

114
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.860
So you are supposed to be surrounded in these beautiful

115
00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.630
creatures that look over you and that you love back

116
00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:18.750
and you and your family, your husband, Max,

117
00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.520
and everything you're doing and you put out in the world,

118
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.260
you're such a part of the majesty of human capacity

119
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.270
and what good people and how they make their mark

120
00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.360
on this planet.

121
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.150
And I thank you so much for being here, Iman,

122
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.768
and kicking off this Beauty Week.

123
00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:39.430
It can't be a greater honor than to do it with you.

124
00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:40.293
Thank you.

125
00:05:41.288 --> 00:05:42.779
Thank you very much.

126
00:05:42.779 --> 00:05:43.612
Thank you so much.

127
00:05:43.612 --> 00:05:46.195 line:15%
(upbeat music)

